## Outreach history and suppression (resolved 2026-08-09)

`VERIFIED` Bounce notices in `lexiconatlas@gmail.com` proved that real outreach went
out on 2026-08-03/04, while no send ledger existed on disk. The system had no record
of having contacted anyone.

Two businesses were emailed and both permanently bounced. In each case the recipient
mail server refuses connections outright, which matches their audit grades (both D,
41/100):

| Address | Outcome |
| --- | --- |
| `service@ashevillelawncare.com` | Hard bounce 2026-08-04 |
| `info@ashevillepressurewashing.com` | Delayed 08-05 and 08-06, hard bounce 08-07 |

Actions taken, in this order and for this reason:

1. Both addresses were suppressed in `dnc.local.json` first, as an immediate guardrail.
   Addresses only, not domains, so a business that repairs its mail server is not
   blocked forever.
2. `outreach_ledger.py` now keeps sends and suppressions in Firestore, because local
   files cannot survive a Cloud Run execution. Every read is fail-closed: if the
   record cannot be trusted, the dispatcher refuses to send rather than treating an
   empty answer as "nobody has been contacted".
3. `reconstruct_outreach_history.py` then wrote the recoverable history into that
   durable store. Reconstruction had to come after step 2, or the recovered record
   would have landed in the very file that gets destroyed. Rows are marked
   `reconstructed` and carry no real message hash, because a bounce proves contact
   but not content.

`VERIFIED` Both addresses now read back as suppressed and as previously-contacted
from local files and from Firestore, and a simulated backend outage refuses instead
of returning an empty set.

